{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-975c1a173f5a8573","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstatus: completed\n3\tspec: [004-bug-job-failure-reason]\n4\tsummary: 'Split BackoffLimitExceeded from DeadlineExceeded in the job failure classifier: added pod_oom_killed reason, pod-resolution reason composition (reason + exit code in published text), internal regression tests, and CHANGELOG entry'\n5\texecution_id: agent-task-executor-failreason-exec-005-spec-004-bug-job-failure-reason\n6\tdark-factory-version: dev\n7\tcreated: \"2026-08-31T23:55:00Z\"\n8\tqueued: \"2026-08-31T22:03:30Z\"\n9\tstarted: \"2026-08-31T22:03:31Z\"\n10\tcompleted: \"2026-08-31T22:11:18Z\"\n11\tbranch: dark-factory/bug-job-failure-reason\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t<summary>\n15\t- A Job that exhausted its retry backoff limit is no longer mislabeled `deadline_exceeded`; only a genuine active-deadline kill still reports `deadline_exceeded`.\n16\t- A new machine-readable reason value `pod_oom_killed` is added to the closed reason set for OOM-killed pods, so operators can grep for OOM kills specifically.\n17\t- For backoff-limit failures, the published failure text now carries the pod's actual termination reason and exit code (e.g. `OOMKilled`/`137`) — no manual `kubectl get pod -o json` needed.\n18\t- Non-OOM pod deaths under a backoff-limit failure surface their own termination reason + exit code (e.g. `Error`/`1`) under the existing generic pod-failure reason, never `deadline_exceeded` and never `pod_oom_killed`.\n19\t- When the failed pod is no longer resolvable (GC'd or lister not synced), the Job still publishes a distinct non-deadline reason, just without pod detail.\n20\t- The executor log line and the vault task's `## Failure` body show the same specific reason string.\n21\t- The two existing tests that pinned the wrong `deadline_exceeded` mapping are corrected; new regression tests lock the OOM path and the non-OOM path.\n22\t- A `## Unreleased` CHANGELOG section is created for this fix.\n23\t</summary>\n24\t\n25\t<objective>\n26\tFix the job-failure classifier so a Job that hit `BackoffLimitExceeded` publishes a distinct pod-failure reason that surfaces the pod's terminated reason + exit code (with a dedicated `pod_oom_killed` for OOM kills), never the misleading `deadline_exceeded`, while leaving the genuine deadline path unchanged. Motivating incident (2026-08-16): a pod OOM-killed at ~6 min of a 30-min budget was reported as `deadline_exceeded`, and three genuinely different failures were all logged identically, misdirecting a post-mortem that tallied 82 Jobs as quota-starved.\n27\t</objective>\n28\t\n29\t<context>\n30\tRead `/home/node/.claude/CLAUDE.md` for project conventions (Ginkgo/Gomega v2, `github.com/bborbe/errors` wrapping, counterfeiter mocks, glog V(n) gating, coverage ≥80% for new code).\n31\t\n32\tRead these files fully before changing anything:\n33\t- `/workspace/pkg/job_watcher.go` — `JobFailureReason` (lines ~249-267), `HandleJob` (lines ~139-162), `handleTerminal` (lines ~164-182), `publishSyntheticFailure` (lines ~184-197), `PodLister()` (lines ~131-137), the `podLister atomic.Pointer[corev1listers.PodLister]` field (line 65), and the existing `classifyPodFailure`/`ownerJobName` helpers (lines ~320-363). Note `ownerJobName(pod)` already returns the owning Job name from a Pod's ownerRefs — reuse it.\n34\t- `/workspace/pkg/zombie_reason.go` — the closed `ZombieReason` set and its doc contract (\"Adding a new value requires updating this list and the documentation\").\n35\t- `/workspace/pkg/job_watcher_test.go` — the two `BackoffLimitExceeded` assertions to correct (HandleJob path ~line 240, direct-mapping path ~line 271) and the two `DeadlineExceeded` assertions that must stay (lines ~220 and ~262).\n36\t- `/workspace/pkg/zombie_reason_test.go` — the per-value `String()` assertions to extend.\n37\t- `/workspace/pkg/zombie_sweeper_test.go` — the informer-indexer pattern for building a test `PodLister` without starting informers (search `podInformer.GetIndexer().Add` / `informerFactory.Core().V1().Pods().Lister()`); mirror it in the new internal test file below.\n38\t- `/workspace/pkg/result_publisher.go` — `PublishFailure` (lines ~171-230) writes the reason verbatim into the vault `## Failure` body's `- **Reason:**` line; this is why the composed reason string must keep the greppable value as its leading token.\n39\t\n40\tRelevant docs (in-container paths):\n41\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/go-testing-guide.md` — Ginkgo/Gomega, external test packages, coverage ≥80%.\n42\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/go-error-wrapping-guide.md` — never `fmt.Errorf`; `fmt.Sprintf` for string building is an established pattern (see `result_publisher.go`).\n43\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/go-glog-guide.md` — `glog.V(2)` for per-failure events, `glog.Errorf` for lister lookup errors.\n44\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/changelog-guide.md` — `## Unreleased` format and prefix rules.\n45\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/definition-of-done.md` — coverage rules for new code.\n46\t</context>\n47\t\n48\t<requirements>\n49\t1. Add the new reason value in `/workspace/pkg/zombie_reason.go`. Add `ZombieReasonPodOOMKilled` to the const block after `ZombieReasonTypeMismatch`, keeping the existing alignment style (gofmt normalizes):\n50\t   ```go\n51\t   ZombieReasonPodOOMKilled      ZombieReason = \"pod_oom_killed\"\n52\t   ```\n53\t   Update the package doc comment (the `ZombieReason` block comment) so it states the new contract: OOM kills use the dedicated `pod_oom_killed` value so operators can grep for OOM specifically; other non-zero pod terminations fall back to the generic `pod_crash_no_stdout`. Keep the existing \"adding a value requires updating this list and the documentation\" sentence.\n54\t\n55\t2. Add the matching `String()` assertion in `/workspace/pkg/zombie_reason_test.go`, inside the `Describe(\"String()\", ...)` block (after the `type_mismatch` `It`):\n56\t   ```go\n57\t   It(\"returns pod_oom_killed for ZombieReasonPodOOMKilled\", func() {\n58\t       Expect(pkg.ZombieReasonPodOOMKilled.String()).To(Equal(\"pod_oom_killed\"))\n59\t   })\n60\t   ```\n61\t\n62\t3. Split the classifier in `/workspace/pkg/job_watcher.go`. Replace the body of `JobFailureReason` so `BackoffLimitExceeded` maps to the generic pod-failure reason instead of `ZombieReasonDeadlineExceeded`, and update its doc comment. Do NOT merge the two conditions — a `DeadlineExceeded` condition always yields `deadline_exceeded`, even if a `BackoffLimitExceeded` condition is also present. New form:\n63\t   ```go\n64\t   // JobFailureReason maps a failed Job's conditions to a ZombieReason. Returns\n65\t   // ZombieReasonDeadlineExceeded when a Failed condition has Reason\n66\t   // \"DeadlineExceeded\" (the kubelet killed the pod for running past\n67\t   // activeDeadlineSeconds). Returns the generic pod-failure reason\n68\t   // ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout for \"BackoffLimitExceeded\" (the Job exhausted\n69\t   // its retry backoff limit — a distinct reason from a deadline kill; HandleJob\n70\t   // resolves the pod's terminated reason + exit code and surfaces them in the\n71\t   // published reason) and for any other Failed condition (the pod terminated\n72\t   // non-zero and no AgentResult was observed; the Job-condition informer only\n73\t   // fires AFTER terminal state, so absence of an AgentResult is implicit at this\n74\t   // point).\n75\t   func JobFailureReason(job *batchv1.Job) ZombieReason {\n76\t       for _, c := range job.Status.Conditions {\n77\t           if c.Type == batchv1.JobFailed && c.Status == corev1.ConditionTrue {\n78\t               switch c.Reason {\n79\t               case \"DeadlineExceeded\":\n80\t                   return ZombieReasonDeadlineExceeded\n81\t               case \"BackoffLimitExceeded\":\n82\t                   return ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout\n83\t               }\n84\t           }\n85\t       }\n86\t       return ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout\n87\t   }\n88\t   ```\n89\t\n90\t4. Add a package-level condition helper near `IsJobFailed`:\n91\t   ```go\n92\t   // isBackoffLimitExceeded reports whether the Job's terminal Failed condition\n93\t   // has Reason \"BackoffLimitExceeded\" (the Job exhausted its retry backoff\n94\t   // limit).\n95\t   func isBackoffLimitExceeded(job *batchv1.Job) bool {\n96\t       for _, c := range job.Status.Conditions {\n97\t           if c.Type == batchv1.JobFailed && c.Status == corev1.ConditionTrue && c.Reason == \"BackoffLimitExceeded\" {\n98\t               return true\n99\t           }\n100\t       }\n101\t       return false\n102\t   }\n103\t   ```\n104\t\n105\t5. Add a package-level pod-state helper:\n106\t   ```go\n107\t   // hasNonZeroTerminatedContainer reports whether any container terminated with\n108\t   // a non-zero exit code.\n109\t   func hasNonZeroTerminatedContainer(pod *corev1.Pod) bool {\n110\t       for _, cs := range pod.Status.ContainerStatuses {\n111\t           if cs.State.Terminated != nil && cs.State.Terminated.ExitCode != 0 {\n112\t               return true\n113\t           }\n114\t       }\n115\t       return false\n116\t   }\n117\t   ```\n118\t\n119\t6. Add the pod-resolution method on `*jobWatcher`. It resolves the Job's most recently created pod from the shared pod lister (matched by Job owner reference via the existing `ownerJobName` helper) and returns its `.state.terminated.reason` and `exitCode`. Every failure path returns `ok=false` with no side effects — never a panic:\n120\t   ```go\n121\t   // terminalPodDetail returns the terminated reason and exit code of the Job's\n122\t   // most recently created pod that has a non-zero terminated container, matched\n123\t   // by Job owner reference. ok is false when the pod lister is not yet synced,\n124\t   // the lister lookup fails, or no owned pod carries a non-zero terminated\n125\t   // container.\n126\t   func (w *jobWatcher) terminalPodDetail(job *batchv1.Job) (terminatedReason string, exitCode int32, ok bool) {\n127\t       lister := w.PodLister()\n128\t       if lister == nil {\n129\t           return \"\", 0, false\n130\t       }\n131\t       pods, err := lister.Pods(job.Namespace).List(labels.Everything())\n132\t       if err != nil {\n133\t           glog.Errorf(\"job watcher: list pods for job %s/%s: %v\", job.Namespace, job.Name, err)\n134\t           return \"\", 0, false\n135\t       }\n136\t       var best *corev1.Pod\n137\t       for _, pod := range pods {\n138\t           if ownerJobName(pod) != job.Name {\n139\t               continue\n140\t           }\n141\t           if !hasNonZeroTerminatedContainer(pod) {\n142\t               continue\n143\t           }\n144\t           if best == nil || pod.CreationTimestamp.After(best.CreationTimestamp.Time) {\n145\t               best = pod\n146\t           }\n147\t       }\n148\t       if best == nil {\n149\t           return \"\", 0, false\n150\t       }\n151\t       for _, cs := range best.Status.ContainerStatuses {\n152\t           if cs.State.Terminated != nil && cs.State.Terminated.ExitCode != 0 {\n153\t               return cs.State.Terminated.Reason, cs.State.Terminated.ExitCode, true\n154\t           }\n155\t       }\n156\t       return \"\", 0, false\n157\t   }\n158\t   ```\n159\t\n160\t7. Add the reason-composition methods on `*jobWatcher`. The published reason string keeps the machine-readable `ZombieReason` value as its leading token and appends the pod detail in parentheses; the spec leaves the exact detail format to you and this is the chosen format. Use `fmt.Sprintf` (established in `result_publisher.go`), never `fmt.Errorf`:\n161\t   ```go\n162\t   // failureReason returns the reason string to publish for a failed Job. A\n163\t   // BackoffLimitExceeded failure surfaces the pod's terminated reason + exit\n164\t   // code when the pod is still resolvable (see backoffFailureReason); every\n165\t   // other failure publishes the bare ZombieReason value.\n166\t   func (w *jobWatcher) failureReason(job *batchv1.Job) string {\n167\t       reason := JobFailureReason(job)\n168\t       if reason == ZombieReasonDeadlineExceeded || !isBackoffLimitExceeded(job) {\n169\t           return reason.String()\n170\t       }\n171\t       return w.backoffFailureReason(job, reason)\n172\t   }\n173\t\n174\t   // backoffFailureReason classifies a BackoffLimitExceeded Job using its\n175\t   // resolved pod's terminal state: the dedicated pod_oom_killed for an OOMKilled\n176\t   // pod, else the generic pod-failure reason, always appending the terminated\n177\t   // reason + exit code when a pod is resolvable so operators see the cause\n178\t   // without inspecting the pod.\n179\t   func (w *jobWatcher) backoffFailureReason(job *batchv1.Job, base ZombieReason) string {\n180\t       terminatedReason, exitCode, ok := w.terminalPodDetail(job)\n181\t       if !ok {\n182\t           return base.String()\n183\t       }\n184\t       if terminatedReason == \"OOMKilled\" {\n185\t           return fmt.Sprintf(\"%s (%s/%d)\", ZombieReasonPodOOMKilled, terminatedReason, exitCode)\n186\t       }\n187\t       return fmt.Sprintf(\"%s (%s/%d)\", base.String(), terminatedReason, exitCode)\n188\t   }\n189\t   ```\n190\t   Add `\"fmt\"` to the stdlib import group and `\"k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/labels\"` to the k8s import group in this file. Do not worry about final import ordering — the precommit `format` step (goimports-reviser) normalizes it.\n191\t\n192\t8. Wire the composition into `HandleJob` and the publish path (no interface change to `ResultPublisher`):\n193\t   - In `HandleJob`, change `reason := JobFailureReason(job)` to `reason := w.failureReason(job)`. The variable becomes a `string`; the existing `glog.V(2).Infof(\"job %s/%s failed (task %s): %s\", job.Namespace, job.Name, taskID, reason)` and `w.handleTerminal(ctx, taskID, job, reason, true)` calls keep working unchanged. This makes the executor log line show the same specific reason as the published failure.\n194\t   - Change `handleTerminal`'s `reason ZombieReason` parameter to `reason string` (no other behavior change).\n195\t   - Change `publishSyntheticFailure`'s `reason ZombieReason` parameter to `reason string`, and change the `PublishFailure` call from `reason.String()` to `reason` — the reason string flows through `PublishFailure`'s existing `(ctx, task, jobName, reason string)` signature as-is.\n196\t   - Do NOT change `ResultPublisher` or the `PublishFailure` signature.\n197\t   - Spec Failure Mode row 5 (executor crashes mid-publish after classifying) is pre-existing behavior, NOT new work: `PublishFailure` is at-least-once via Kafka and its dedupe (keyed on job name) in `/workspace/pkg/result_publisher.go` already absorbs re-delivery. Do not modify that dedupe code.\n198\t\n199\t9. Correct the two wrong `BackoffLimitExceeded` tests in `/workspace/pkg/job_watcher_test.go`:\n200\t   - HandleJob path (~line 240): rename the spec to \"maps BackoffLimitExceeded job condition to pod_crash_no_stdout (no resolvable pod)\" and change the final assertion from `Expect(calledReason).To(Equal(\"deadline_exceeded\"))` to `Expect(calledReason).To(Equal(\"pod_crash_no_stdout\"))`. This test runs against a watcher whose pod lister was never populated (`Run()` is never called, so `PodLister()` returns nil) — it exercises the nil-lister path of `terminalPodDetail` and proves the reason is distinct from `deadline_exceeded`.\n201\t   - Direct-mapping path (~line 271): rename to \"returns pod_crash_no_stdout for BackoffLimitExceeded\" and change the assertion from `pkg.ZombieReasonDeadlineExceeded` to `pkg.ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout`.\n202\t   - Do NOT touch the two `DeadlineExceeded` assertions (lines ~220 and ~262) — they must keep asserting `deadline_exceeded`.\n203\t\n204\t10. Add an internal test file `/workspace/pkg/job_watcher_internal_test.go` in `package pkg` (this pairs an internal test file with the existing external one, matching the repo's `main_test.go` + `main_internal_test.go` precedent). It exists because the OOM/backoff pod-resolution path needs a populated pod lister on the real watcher, and `Run()` is never called in tests so `PodLister()` would otherwise return nil.\n205\t    - Helper `newTestPodLister(pods ...*corev1.Pod) corev1listers.PodLister`: create `fake.NewSimpleClientset()`, then `k8sinformers.NewSharedInformerFactoryWithOptions(fakeClient, 0, k8sinformers.WithNamespace(\"test-ns\"))`, add each pod with `_ = informerFactory.Core().V1().Pods().Informer().GetIndexer().Add(pod)`, and return `informerFactory.Core().V1().Pods().Lister()` — mirror the exact pattern in `/workspace/pkg/zombie_sweeper_test.go`.\n206\t    - Helper `setPodLister(w JobWatcher, pods ...*corev1.Pod)`: `lister := newTestPodLister(pods...)` then `w.(*jobWatcher).podLister.Store(&lister)`.\n207\t    - `BeforeEach`: `ctx = context.Background()`, `fakePublisher = &mocks.FakeResultPublisher{}`, `taskStore = NewTaskStore()`, `watcher = NewJobWatcher(fake.NewSimpleClientset(), \"test-ns\", taskStore, fakePublisher)`, and a `testTask`/`testTaskID` pair mirroring the external file's setup (task type `lib.Task` from `github.com/bborbe/agent`, frontmatter with `status`/`assignee`).\n208\t    - Helper `makeJob(name, taskID string, conditions ...batchv1.JobCondition) *batchv1.Job`: same shape as the external file (namespace `\"test-ns\"`, label `agent.benjamin-borbe.de/task-id`, `Status.Conditions` from args).\n209\t    - Helper `backoffCondition() batchv1.JobCondition`: `{Type: batchv1.JobFailed, Status: corev1.ConditionTrue, Reason: \"BackoffLimitExceeded\", Message: \"Job has reached the specified backoff limit\"}`.\n210\t    - Helper `makeOwnedTerminatedPod(name, jobName, taskID, terminatedReason string, exitCode int32, createdAt string) *corev1.Pod`: namespace `\"test-ns\"`, labels `{\"agent.benjamin-borbe.de/task-id\": taskID}`, `OwnerReferences: []metav1.OwnerReference{{Kind: \"Job\", Name: jobName}}`, `Status.Phase = corev1.PodFailed`, `Status.ContainerStatuses = []corev1.ContainerStatus{{State: corev1.ContainerState{Terminated: &corev1.ContainerStateTerminated{Reason: terminatedReason, ExitCode: exitCode}}}}`, and `CreationTimestamp = metav1.NewTime(t)` parsed from `createdAt` (RFC3339) when non-empty.\n211\t    - Then add these Ginkgo specs (each: `taskStore.Store(testTaskID, testTask)`, `watcher.HandleJob(ctx, job)`, assert `fakePublisher.PublishFailureCallCount()` is 1, capture `_, _, _, calledReason := fakePublisher.PublishFailureArgsForCall(0)`):\n212\t      a. OOMKilled pod resolvable → `Expect(calledReason).To(Equal(\"pod_oom_killed (OOMKilled/137)\"))` and `Expect(calledReason).NotTo(ContainSubstring(\"deadline_exceeded\"))`. This one spec satisfies ACs 2a, 3, and 4.\n213\t      b. Non-OOM terminated pod (`Error`/exit `1`) → `Expect(calledReason).To(Equal(\"pod_crash_no_stdout (Error/1)\"))`, plus `NotTo(ContainSubstring(\"deadline_exceeded\"))` and `NotTo(ContainSubstring(\"pod_oom_killed\"))`. Satisfies AC 2b.\n214\t      c. Two pods owned by the same job with different terminated reasons (earlier `Error`/`1`, later `OOMKilled`/`137` — distinct `createdAt` timestamps) → `Expect(calledReason).To(Equal(\"pod_oom_killed (OOMKilled/137)\"))`, proving the terminal (last) pod's state is used (spec Failure Mode row 4).\n215\t      d. Empty lister cache (no pods) → `Expect(calledReason).To(Equal(\"pod_crash_no_stdout\"))` with no detail (spec Failure Mode rows 1 and 3).\n216\t      e. An owned pod with no non-zero terminated container (e.g. `corev1.PodRunning` with no terminated state) → `Expect(calledReason).To(Equal(\"pod_crash_no_stdout\"))` with no detail.\n217\t\n218\t11. Add the CHANGELOG entry in `/workspace/CHANGELOG.md`: create `## Unreleased` directly under the `# Changelog` heading (none exists at HEAD v0.8.0) with one bullet following the changelog-guide format:\n219\t    ```\n220\t    ## Unreleased\n221\t\n222\t    - fix: split `BackoffLimitExceeded` from `DeadlineExceeded` in the job failure classifier — a Job that exhausted its backoff limit now publishes the pod's terminated reason + exit code (dedicated `pod_oom_killed` for OOM kills) instead of a misleading `deadline_exceeded`\n223\t    ```\n224\t\n225\t12. Do NOT touch `pkg/zombie_sweeper.go`'s `classify` deadline fallback — the spec's Non-goal explicitly defers it as a follow-up.\n226\t</requirements>\n227\t\n228\t<constraints>\n229\t- Repo conventions are frozen: Ginkgo/Gomega v2 tests (no stdlib table tests), `github.com/bborbe/errors` wrapping (never `fmt.Errorf`), counterfeiter mocks for any new dependency, glog `V(n)` gating.\n230\t- `ZombieReason` is a closed machine-readable set (`pkg/zombie_reason.go`). The new `pod_oom_killed` value is added deliberately and documented in the doc comment, which states that adding a value requires updating the list and the documentation.\n231\t- Do NOT change `PublishFailure`'s interface or signature — the reason string flows through as-is. You may change the internal `handleTerminal`/`publishSyntheticFailure` parameter type from `ZombieReason` to `string`.\n232\t- Assumption: the failed Job's pod is usually still resolvable at `HandleJob` time (`TTLSecondsAfterFinished` keeps it until TTL expiry). When it is not (pod GC'd / lister not synced / lister nil), the Job-condition path still publishes a distinct pod-failure reason without pod detail — never `deadline_exceeded`.\n233\t- The two existing `DeadlineExceeded` assertions in `pkg/job_watcher_test.go` stay; the two `BackoffLimitExceeded` assertions that pin the wrong behavior are updated.\n234\t- CHANGELOG: add an `## Unreleased` bullet for this fix (creating the section if absent — none exists at HEAD v0.8.0).\n235\t- Non-goals are load-bearing: do NOT raise the Job memory limit, do NOT fix the GitHub App `workflows`-scope permission, do NOT touch the resync `not in task store` warning noise, do NOT touch `zombie_sweeper.go`'s `classify` deadline fallback.\n236\t- Do NOT commit — dark-factory handles git.\n237\t- Existing tests must still pass.\n238\t</constraints>\n239\t\n240\t<verification>\n241\tRun `make test` iteratively after each meaningful change (fast feedback loop), then `make precommit` ONCE at the very end.\n242\t\n243\t- `make test` — exits 0.\n244\t- `make precommit` — exits 0.\n245\t- `go test -mod=mod -coverprofile=/tmp/cover.out ./pkg/... && go tool cover -func=/tmp/cover.out` — confirm the new functions (`failureReason`, `backoffFailureReason`, `terminalPodDetail`, `isBackoffLimitExceeded`, `hasNonZeroTerminatedContainer`) are each exercised by at least one test (≥80% statement coverage for new code).\n246\t- `grep -n 'BackoffLimitExceeded' pkg/job_watcher.go` — returns at least one line that does NOT map to `ZombieReasonDeadlineExceeded` (acceptance-criterion evidence).\n247\t- `grep -n 'pod_oom_killed' pkg/zombie_reason.go pkg/zombie_reason_test.go pkg/job_watcher.go pkg/job_watcher_internal_test.go` — shows the new value in the closed set, its `String()` assertion, and its production usage.\n248\t\n249\tDo NOT run `docker`, `make build`, `kubectl`, or `dark-factory` commands in this prompt — those are operator-executable and belong on the spec's verification ladder.\n250\t</verification>\n251\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a883ed86f0a5df7d","session_id":"649c3b71-f1fd-46a9-a844-34ab62b5da5e","uuid":"244da20b-aa19-4653-8140-849a6ff3e6ff","timestamp":"2026-09-03T17:46:27.737Z","subagent_type":"dark-factory:prompt-creator","task_description":"Generate prompts from spec 002"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-97e9f1bddad0dd81","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Copyright (c) 2026 Benjamin Borbe All rights reserved.\n2\t// Use of this source code is governed by a BSD-style\n3\t// license that can be found in the LICENSE file.\n4\t\n5\tpackage spawner\n6\t\n7\timport (\n8\t\t\"context\"\n9\t\n10\t\tlib \"github.com/bborbe/agent\"\n11\t\t\"github.com/bborbe/errors\"\n12\t\tk8s \"github.com/bborbe/k8s\"\n13\t\tlibtime \"github.com/bborbe/time\"\n14\t\t\"github.com/golang/glog\"\n15\t\t\"gopkg.in/yaml.v3\"\n16\t\tbatchv1 \"k8s.io/api/batch/v1\"\n17\t\tcorev1 \"k8s.io/api/core/v1\"\n18\t\tk8serrors \"k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/api/errors\"\n19\t\t\"k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/api/resource\"\n20\t\tmetav1 \"k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/apis/meta/v1\"\n21\t\t\"k8s.io/client-go/kubernetes\"\n22\t\n23\t\tpkg \"github.com/bborbe/agent-task-executor/pkg\"\n24\t)\n25\t\n26\t// taskIDLabelKey labels a spawned Job with the task UUID so IsJobActive\n27\t// can look it up. Must match the selector used in IsJobActive.\n28\tconst taskIDLabelKey = \"agent.benjamin-borbe.de/task-id\"\n29\t\n30\t// assigneeLabelKey labels a spawned Job with the agent assignee so\n31\t// CountActiveJobs can size concurrency per agent rather than globally.\n32\t// Different agents have very different runtimes and resource profiles, so a\n33\t// single fleet-wide limit would throttle cheap agents to protect expensive ones.\n34\tconst assigneeLabelKey = \"agent.benjamin-borbe.de/assignee\"\n35\t\n36\t//counterfeiter:generate -o ../../mocks/job_spawner.go --fake-name FakeJobSpawner . JobSpawner\n37\t\n38\t// JobSpawner creates a K8s Job for a task.\n39\ttype JobSpawner interface {\n40\t\t// SpawnJob creates a K8s Job for the task and returns the job name.\n41\t\t// Returns the job name even when the job already exists (idempotent).\n42\t\tSpawnJob(ctx context.Context, task lib.Task, config pkg.AgentConfiguration) (string, error)\n43\t\t// IsJobActive returns true if an active (not completed/failed) K8s Job exists\n44\t\t// for the given task identifier. Uses the agent.benjamin-borbe.de/task-id\n45\t\t// label set by SpawnJob.\n46\t\tIsJobActive(ctx context.Context, taskIdentifier lib.TaskIdentifier) (bool, error)\n47\t\t// CountActiveJobs returns the number of non-terminal K8s Jobs currently\n48\t\t// running for the given assignee. Uses the agent.benjamin-borbe.de/assignee\n49\t\t// label set by SpawnJob. Jobs spawned before that label existed are not\n50\t\t// counted, so the cap under-counts during the rollout and never over-counts.\n51\t\tCountActiveJobs(ctx context.Context, assignee string) (int, error)\n52\t}\n53\t\n54\t// NewJobSpawner creates a new JobSpawner backed by the K8s batch/v1 API.\n55\t// jobTTLSecondsAfterFinished controls how long completed Job pods survive\n56\t// before Kubernetes' TTL controller garbage-collects them.\n57\t// jobKafkaClientCertSecret and jobKafkaCaCertSecret are the names of K8s secrets\n58\t// to mount into spawned Jobs for Kafka mTLS; empty strings mean no cert mounting.\n59\tfunc NewJobSpawner(\n60\t\tkubeClient kubernetes.Interface,\n61\t\tnamespace k8s.Namespace,\n62\t\tkafkaBrokers string,\n63\t\tbranch string,\n64\t\ttopicPrefix string,\n65\t\tcurrentDateTimeGetter libtime.CurrentDateTimeGetter,\n66\t\tjobTTLSecondsAfterFinished int32,\n67\t\tjobKafkaClientCertSecret string,\n68\t\tjobKafkaCaCertSecret string,\n69\t) JobSpawner {\n70\t\treturn &jobSpawner{\n71\t\t\tkubeClient:                 kubeClient,\n72\t\t\tnamespace:                  namespace,\n73\t\t\tkafkaBrokers:               kafkaBrokers,\n74\t\t\tbranch:                     branch,\n75\t\t\ttopicPrefix:                topicPrefix,\n76\t\t\tcurrentDateTimeGetter:      currentDateTimeGetter,\n77\t\t\tjobTTLSecondsAfterFinished: jobTTLSecondsAfterFinished,\n78\t\t\tjobKafkaClientCertSecret:   jobKafkaClientCertSecret,\n79\t\t\tjobKafkaCaCertSecret:       jobKafkaCaCertSecret,\n80\t\t}\n81\t}\n82\t\n83\t// jobSpawner implements JobSpawner by creating batch/v1 Jobs via the K8s client.\n84\ttype jobSpawner struct {\n85\t\tkubeClient                 kubernetes.Interface\n86\t\tnamespace                  k8s.Namespace\n87\t\tkafkaBrokers               string\n88\t\tbranch                     string\n89\t\ttopicPrefix                string\n90\t\tcurrentDateTimeGetter      libtime.CurrentDateTimeGetter\n91\t\tjobTTLSecondsAfterFinished int32\n92\t\tjobKafkaClientCertSecret   string\n93\t\tjobKafkaCaCertSecret       string\n94\t}\n95\t\n96\tfunc (s *jobSpawner) SpawnJob(\n97\t\tctx context.Context,\n98\t\ttask lib.Task,\n99\t\tconfig pkg.AgentConfiguration,\n100\t) (string, error) {\n101\t\tassignee := task.Frontmatter.Assignee().String()\n102\t\tnow := s.currentDateTimeGetter.Now()\n103\t\tjobName := jobNameFromTask(assignee, task.TaskIdentifier, now)\n104\t\n105\t\tenvBuilder, err := s.buildJobEnvBuilder(ctx, task, config)\n106\t\tif err != nil {\n107\t\t\treturn \"\", err\n108\t\t}\n109\t\n110\t\tcontainerBuilder := k8s.NewContainerBuilder()\n111\t\tcontainerBuilder.SetName(k8s.Name(\"agent\"))\n112\t\tcontainerBuilder.SetImage(config.Image)\n113\t\tcontainerBuilder.SetEnvBuilder(envBuilder)\n114\t\tapplyCPUMemoryResources(config, containerBuilder)\n115\t\n116\t\tpodSpecBuilder := k8s.NewPodSpecBuilder()\n117\t\n118\t\tif err := applyVolumeMount(ctx, config, containerBuilder, podSpecBuilder); err != nil {\n119\t\t\treturn \"\", err\n120\t\t}\n121\t\n122\t\tcontainersBuilder := k8s.NewContainersBuilder()\n123\t\tcontainersBuilder.SetContainerBuilders([]k8s.HasBuildContainer{containerBuilder})\n124\t\n125\t\tpodSpecBuilder.SetContainersBuilder(containersBuilder)\n126\t\tpodSpecBuilder.SetRestartPolicy(corev1.RestartPolicyNever)\n127\t\tpodSpecBuilder.SetImagePullSecrets([]string{imagePullSecretName(config)})\n128\t\n129\t\tobjectMetaBuilder := k8s.NewObjectMetaBuilder()\n130\t\tobjectMetaBuilder.SetName(k8s.Name(jobName))\n131\t\tobjectMetaBuilder.SetNamespace(s.namespace)\n132\t\n133\t\tjobBuilder := k8s.NewJobBuilder()\n134\t\tjobBuilder.SetObjectMetaBuild(objectMetaBuilder)\n135\t\tjobBuilder.SetPodSpecBuilder(podSpecBuilder)\n136\t\tjobBuilder.SetBackoffLimit(0)\n137\t\tjobBuilder.SetTTLSecondsAfterFinished(s.jobTTLSecondsAfterFinished)\n138\t\tjobBuilder.SetApp(\"agent\")\n139\t\tjobBuilder.SetComponent(string(task.TaskIdentifier))\n140\t\n141\t\tjob, err := jobBuilder.Build(ctx)\n142\t\tif err != nil {\n143\t\t\treturn \"\", errors.Wrapf(ctx, err, \"build job for task %s\", task.TaskIdentifier)\n144\t\t}\n145\t\n146\t\tapplyTaskIDLabel(task.TaskIdentifier, job)\n147\t\tapplyAssigneeLabel(config.Assignee, job)\n148\t\tapplySecretEnvFrom(config, job)\n149\t\tapplyEphemeralStorage(config, job)\n150\t\ts.applyKafkaCertVolumes(job)\n151\t\tif config.PriorityClassName != \"\" {\n152\t\t\tjob.Spec.Template.Spec.PriorityClassName = config.PriorityClassName\n153\t\t}\n154\t\tapplyActiveDeadlineSeconds(config, jobName, task.TaskIdentifier, job)\n155\t\n156\t\t_, err = s.kubeClient.BatchV1().\n157\t\t\tJobs(s.namespace.String()).\n158\t\t\tCreate(ctx, job, metav1.CreateOptions{})\n159\t\tif err != nil {\n160\t\t\tif k8serrors.IsAlreadyExists(err) {\n161\t\t\t\tglog.V(2).\n162\t\t\t\t\tInfof(\"job %s already exists for task %s, treating as success\", jobName, task.TaskIdentifier)\n163\t\t\t\treturn jobName, nil\n164\t\t\t}\n165\t\t\treturn \"\", errors.Wrapf(\n166\t\t\t\tctx,\n167\t\t\t\terr,\n168\t\t\t\t\"create job %s for task %s failed\",\n169\t\t\t\tjobName,\n170\t\t\t\ttask.TaskIdentifier,\n171\t\t\t)\n172\t\t}\n173\t\tglog.V(2).\n174\t\t\tInfof(\"created job %s for task %s with image %s\", jobName, task.TaskIdentifier, config.Image)\n175\t\treturn jobName, nil\n176\t}\n177\t\n178\tfunc (s *jobSpawner) IsJobActive(\n179\t\tctx context.Context,\n180\t\ttaskIdentifier lib.TaskIdentifier,\n181\t) (bool, error) {\n182\t\tlabelSelector := taskIDLabelKey + \"=\" + string(taskIdentifier)\n183\t\tjobs, err := s.kubeClient.BatchV1().Jobs(s.namespace.String()).List(ctx, metav1.ListOptions{\n184\t\t\tLabelSelector: labelSelector,\n185\t\t})\n186\t\tif err != nil {\n187\t\t\treturn false, errors.Wrapf(ctx, err, \"list jobs for task %s\", taskIdentifier)\n188\t\t}\n189\t\tfor _, job := range jobs.Items {\n190\t\t\t// Terminal conditions first. An activeDeadlineSeconds kill sets the\n191\t\t\t// JobFailed condition but leaves .status.failed and .status.active at\n192\t\t\t// zero, so the counter checks below never fire for it. Without this the\n193\t\t\t// Job reads as active until it is garbage-collected, and every retry\n194\t\t\t// spawn in that window is silently skipped as \"active job exists\" —\n195\t\t\t// which wedged 26 github-update-go tasks on 2026-08-10.\n196\t\t\tif pkg.IsJobFailed(&job) || pkg.IsJobSucceeded(&job) {\n197\t\t\t\tcontinue\n198\t\t\t}\n199\t\t\tif job.Status.Succeeded > 0 {\n200\t\t\t\tcontinue\n201\t\t\t}\n202\t\t\tif job.Status.Failed > 0 && job.Status.Active == 0 {\n203\t\t\t\tcontinue\n204\t\t\t}\n205\t\t\treturn true, nil\n206\t\t}\n207\t\treturn false, nil\n208\t}\n209\t\n210\t// CountActiveJobs returns how many non-terminal Jobs are running for the assignee.\n211\t// Terminal detection matches IsJobActive exactly — conditions first, counters as a\n212\t// fallback — so the two cannot disagree about whether a Job has finished.\n213\tfunc (s *jobSpawner) CountActiveJobs(ctx context.Context, assignee string) (int, error) {\n214\t\tif assignee == \"\" {\n215\t\t\t// Not an error: an unlabelled agent simply has nothing to count. Warn\n216\t\t\t// loudly though, because reaching here with a cap configured means the\n217\t\t\t// cap silently never fires — the exact silent-no-op class of bug this\n218\t\t\t// whole change exists to remove.\n219\t\t\tglog.Warningf(\"CountActiveJobs called with empty assignee; concurrency cap cannot apply\")\n220\t\t\treturn 0, nil\n221\t\t}\n222\t\tlabelSelector := assigneeLabelKey + \"=\" + assignee\n223\t\tjobs, err := s.kubeClient.BatchV1().Jobs(s.namespace.String()).List(ctx, metav1.ListOptions{\n224\t\t\tLabelSelector: labelSelector,\n225\t\t})\n226\t\tif err != nil {\n227\t\t\treturn 0, errors.Wrapf(ctx, err, \"list jobs for assignee %s\", assignee)\n228\t\t}\n229\t\tcount := 0\n230\t\tfor _, job := range jobs.Items {\n231\t\t\tif pkg.IsJobFailed(&job) || pkg.IsJobSucceeded(&job) {\n232\t\t\t\tcontinue\n233\t\t\t}\n234\t\t\tif job.Status.Succeeded > 0 {\n235\t\t\t\tcontinue\n236\t\t\t}\n237\t\t\tif job.Status.Failed > 0 && job.Status.Active == 0 {\n238\t\t\t\tcontinue\n239\t\t\t}\n240\t\t\tcount++\n241\t\t}\n242\t\treturn count, nil\n243\t}\n244\t\n245\t// applyVolumeMount configures a PVC volume mount on the container and pod spec builders\n246\t// when config.VolumeClaim is non-empty. Returns an error if VolumeMountPath is missing.\n247\tfunc applyVolumeMount(\n248\t\tctx context.Context,\n249\t\tconfig pkg.AgentConfiguration,\n250\t\tcontainerBuilder k8s.ContainerBuilder,\n251\t\tpodSpecBuilder k8s.PodSpecBuilder,\n252\t) error {\n253\t\tif config.VolumeClaim == \"\" {\n254\t\t\treturn nil\n255\t\t}\n256\t\tif config.VolumeMountPath == \"\" {\n257\t\t\treturn errors.Errorf(ctx, \"VolumeMountPath required when VolumeClaim is set\")\n258\t\t}\n259\t\tcontainerBuilder.AddVolumeMounts(corev1.VolumeMount{\n260\t\t\tName:      \"agent-data\",\n261\t\t\tMountPath: config.VolumeMountPath,\n262\t\t})\n263\t\tpodSpecBuilder.SetVolumes([]corev1.Volume{\n264\t\t\t{\n265\t\t\t\tName: \"agent-data\",\n266\t\t\t\tVolumeSource: corev1.VolumeSource{\n267\t\t\t\t\tPersistentVolumeClaim: &corev1.PersistentVolumeClaimVolumeSource{\n268\t\t\t\t\t\tClaimName: config.VolumeClaim,\n269\t\t\t\t\t},\n270\t\t\t\t},\n271\t\t\t},\n272\t\t})\n273\t\treturn nil\n274\t}\n275\t\n276\t// imagePullSecretName returns the image pull secret name from the config,\n277\t// falling back to \"docker\" when not set.\n278\tfunc imagePullSecretName(config pkg.AgentConfiguration) string {\n279\t\tif config.ImagePullSecret != \"\" {\n280\t\t\treturn config.ImagePullSecret\n281\t\t}\n282\t\treturn \"docker\"\n283\t}\n284\t\n285\t// applyCPUMemoryResources sets CPU and memory requests/limits on the container builder\n286\t// when the corresponding config values are non-empty. Empty values leave builder defaults untouched.\n287\tfunc applyCPUMemoryResources(config pkg.AgentConfiguration, containerBuilder k8s.ContainerBuilder) {\n288\t\tif config.Resources == nil {\n289\t\t\treturn\n290\t\t}\n291\t\tif v := config.Resources.Requests.CPU; v != \"\" {\n292\t\t\tcontainerBuilder.SetCpuRequest(v)\n293\t\t}\n294\t\tif v := config.Resources.Limits.CPU; v != \"\" {\n295\t\t\tcontainerBuilder.SetCpuLimit(v)\n296\t\t}\n297\t\tif v := config.Resources.Requests.Memory; v != \"\" {\n298\t\t\tcontainerBuilder.SetMemoryRequest(v)\n299\t\t}\n300\t\tif v := config.Resources.Limits.Memory; v != \"\" {\n301\t\t\tcontainerBuilder.SetMemoryLimit(v)\n302\t\t}\n303\t}\n304\t\n305\t// applySecretEnvFrom appends an envFrom secretRef to the first container of the job\n306\t// when config.SecretName is non-empty.\n307\tfunc applySecretEnvFrom(config pkg.AgentConfiguration, job *batchv1.Job) {\n308\t\tif config.SecretName == \"\" {\n309\t\t\treturn\n310\t\t}\n311\t\tjob.Spec.Template.Spec.Containers[0].EnvFrom = append(\n312\t\t\tjob.Spec.Template.Spec.Containers[0].EnvFrom,\n313\t\t\tcorev1.EnvFromSource{\n314\t\t\t\tSecretRef: &corev1.SecretEnvSource{\n315\t\t\t\t\tLocalObjectReference: corev1.LocalObjectReference{\n316\t\t\t\t\t\tName: config.SecretName,\n317\t\t\t\t\t},\n318\t\t\t\t},\n319\t\t\t},\n320\t\t)\n321\t}\n322\t\n323\t// applyEphemeralStorage sets ephemeral-storage as Requests and/or Limits on the\n324\t// first container of the job based on config.Resources.\n325\t// Each value is applied independently — empty means \"leave unset\".\n326\t// The bborbe/k8s container builder does not expose setters for ephemeral-storage,\n327\t// so we patch the built job directly.\n328\tfunc applyEphemeralStorage(config pkg.AgentConfiguration, job *batchv1.Job) {\n329\t\tif config.Resources == nil {\n330\t\t\treturn\n331\t\t}\n332\t\tc := &job.Spec.Template.Spec.Containers[0]\n333\t\tif v := config.Resources.Requests.EphemeralStorage; v != \"\" {\n334\t\t\tif c.Resources.Requests == nil {\n335\t\t\t\tc.Resources.Requests = corev1.ResourceList{}\n336\t\t\t}\n337\t\t\tc.Resources.Requests[corev1.ResourceEphemeralStorage] = resource.MustParse(v)\n338\t\t}\n339\t\tif v := config.Resources.Limits.EphemeralStorage; v != \"\" {\n340\t\t\tif c.Resources.Limits == nil {\n341\t\t\t\tc.Resources.Limits = corev1.ResourceList{}\n342\t\t\t}\n343\t\t\tc.Resources.Limits[corev1.ResourceEphemeralStorage] = resource.MustParse(v)\n344\t\t}\n345\t}\n346\t\n347\t// applyTaskIDLabel sets the agent.benjamin-borbe.de/task-id label on the Job and its pod template\n348\t// so the job informer can look up the owning task by label selector.\n349\tfunc applyTaskIDLabel(taskID lib.TaskIdentifier, job *batchv1.Job) {\n350\t\tif job.Labels == nil {\n351\t\t\tjob.Labels = map[string]string{}\n352\t\t}\n353\t\tjob.Labels[taskIDLabelKey] = string(taskID)\n354\t\tif job.Spec.Template.Labels == nil {\n355\t\t\tjob.Spec.Template.Labels = map[string]string{}\n356\t\t}\n357\t\tjob.Spec.Template.Labels[taskIDLabelKey] = string(taskID)\n358\t}\n359\t\n360\t// applyAssigneeLabel sets the agent.benjamin-borbe.de/assignee label on the Job\n361\t// and its pod template so CountActiveJobs can size concurrency per agent.\n362\tfunc applyAssigneeLabel(assignee string, job *batchv1.Job) {\n363\t\tif assignee == \"\" {\n364\t\t\treturn\n365\t\t}\n366\t\tif job.Labels == nil {\n367\t\t\tjob.Labels = map[string]string{}\n368\t\t}\n369\t\tjob.Labels[assigneeLabelKey] = assignee\n370\t\tif job.Spec.Template.Labels == nil {\n371\t\t\tjob.Spec.Template.Labels = map[string]string{}\n372\t\t}\n373\t\tjob.Spec.Template.Labels[assigneeLabelKey] = assignee\n374\t}\n375\t\n376\t// applyActiveDeadlineSeconds stamps Job.Spec.ActiveDeadlineSeconds from the config's\n377\t// effective zombie job timeout so Kubernetes enforces a hard deadline on every spawned Job.\n378\tfunc applyActiveDeadlineSeconds(\n379\t\tconfig pkg.AgentConfiguration,\n380\t\tjobName string,\n381\t\ttaskID lib.TaskIdentifier,\n382\t\tjob *batchv1.Job,\n383\t) {\n384\t\tdeadline := int64(config.EffectiveZombieJobTimeoutSeconds())\n385\t\tjob.Spec.ActiveDeadlineSeconds = &deadline\n386\t\tglog.V(2).Infof(\"set activeDeadlineSeconds=%d on job %s for task %s\", deadline, jobName, taskID)\n387\t}\n388\t\n389\t// applyKafkaCertVolumes appends three secret volumes (client-cert, client-key, server-cert)\n390\t// to the job's pod spec when BOTH jobKafkaClientCertSecret and jobKafkaCaCertSecret are non-empty.\n391\t// This implements the Kafka mTLS cert mount contract:\n392\t// Each secret volume is mounted at its directory (/client-cert, /client-key,\n393\t// /server-cert) and the KeyToPath item path \"file\" projects the key to the\n394\t// file the bborbe/kafka lib reads (/client-cert/file, /client-key/file,\n395\t// /server-cert/file). Mounting directly at /client-cert/file would make that\n396\t// path a directory containing \"file\" — matches the executor's own cert mount.\n397\t//   - client-cert: secret=<jobKafkaClientCertSecret>, key=user.crt → /client-cert/file\n398\t//   - client-key:  secret=<jobKafkaClientCertSecret>, key=user.key → /client-key/file\n399\t//   - server-cert: secret=<jobKafkaCaCertSecret>,   key=ca.crt   → /server-cert/file\n400\t//\n401\t// When either secret name is empty, the method adds nothing — the spawned Job is unchanged,\n402\t// supporting plaintext-Kafka deployments.\n403\tfunc (s *jobSpawner) applyKafkaCertVolumes(job *batchv1.Job) {\n404\t\tif s.jobKafkaClientCertSecret == \"\" || s.jobKafkaCaCertSecret == \"\" {\n405\t\t\treturn\n406\t\t}\n407\t\tmode := int32(0o440) // decimal 288; owner+group read only\n408\t\tjob.Spec.Template.Spec.Volumes = append(job.Spec.Template.Spec.Volumes,\n409\t\t\tcorev1.Volume{\n410\t\t\t\tName: \"client-cert\",\n411\t\t\t\tVolumeSource: corev1.VolumeSource{\n412\t\t\t\t\tSecret: &corev1.SecretVolumeSource{\n413\t\t\t\t\t\tSecretName:  s.jobKafkaClientCertSecret,\n414\t\t\t\t\t\tItems:       []corev1.KeyToPath{{Key: \"user.crt\", Path: \"file\"}},\n415\t\t\t\t\t\tDefaultMode: &mode,\n416\t\t\t\t\t},\n417\t\t\t\t},\n418\t\t\t},\n419\t\t\tcorev1.Volume{\n420\t\t\t\tName: \"client-key\",\n421\t\t\t\tVolumeSource: corev1.VolumeSource{\n422\t\t\t\t\tSecret: &corev1.SecretVolumeSource{\n423\t\t\t\t\t\tSecretName:  s.jobKafkaClientCertSecret,\n424\t\t\t\t\t\tItems:       []corev1.KeyToPath{{Key: \"user.key\", Path: \"file\"}},\n425\t\t\t\t\t\tDefaultMode: &mode,\n426\t\t\t\t\t},\n427\t\t\t\t},\n428\t\t\t},\n429\t\t\tcorev1.Volume{\n430\t\t\t\tName: \"server-cert\",\n431\t\t\t\tVolumeSource: corev1.VolumeSource{\n432\t\t\t\t\tSecret: &corev1.SecretVolumeSource{\n433\t\t\t\t\t\tSecretName:  s.jobKafkaCaCertSecret,\n434\t\t\t\t\t\tItems:       []corev1.KeyToPath{{Key: \"ca.crt\", Path: \"file\"}},\n435\t\t\t\t\t\tDefaultMode: &mode,\n436\t\t\t\t\t},\n437\t\t\t\t},\n438\t\t\t},\n439\t\t)\n440\t\tcontainer := &job.Spec.Template.Spec.Containers[0]\n441\t\tcontainer.VolumeMounts = append(container.VolumeMounts,\n442\t\t\tcorev1.VolumeMount{Name: \"client-cert\", MountPath: \"/client-cert\"},\n443\t\t\tcorev1.VolumeMount{Name: \"client-key\", MountPath: \"/client-key\"},\n444\t\t\tcorev1.VolumeMount{Name: \"server-cert\", MountPath: \"/server-cert\"},\n445\t\t)\n446\t}\n447\t\n448\t// taskPhaseString returns the string value of the task's phase, or \"\" when absent.\n449\tfunc taskPhaseString(f lib.TaskFrontmatter) string {\n450\t\tif p := f.Phase(); p != nil {\n451\t\t\treturn string(*p)\n452\t\t}\n453\t\treturn \"\"\n454\t}\n455\t\n456\t// taskTypeString returns the string value of the task's task_type, or \"\" when absent.\n457\tfunc taskTypeString(f lib.TaskFrontmatter) string {\n458\t\treturn f.TaskType().String()\n459\t}\n460\t\n461\t// jobNameFromTask returns the K8s Job name for a task: \"{assignee}-{taskID8}-{YYYYMMDDHHMMSS}\".\n462\t// taskID8 is the first 8 chars of the task UUID, included to prevent name collisions\n463\t// between concurrent spawns of different tasks sharing the same assignee and second.\n464\t// If assignee is empty, \"agent\" is used as the default prefix.\n465\t// Job names are DNS-compliant (<=63 chars, [a-z0-9]([-a-z0-9]*[a-z0-9])?).\n466\t// Assignees should be short lowercase strings (e.g. \"claude\", \"backtest-agent\").\n467\tfunc jobNameFromTask(assignee string, taskID lib.TaskIdentifier, now libtime.DateTime) string {\n468\t\tif assignee == \"\" {\n469\t\t\tassignee = \"agent\"\n470\t\t}\n471\t\tid := string(taskID)\n472\t\tif len(id) > 8 {\n473\t\t\tid = id[:8]\n474\t\t}\n475\t\treturn assignee + \"-\" + id + \"-\" + now.UTC().Format(\"20060102150405\")\n476\t}\n477\t\n478\t// buildJobEnvBuilder constructs the env var set for the spawned Job's container.\n479\t// It renders the full markdown for TASK_CONTENT so agents can parse frontmatter\n480\t// fields like clone_url, ref, and base_ref.\n481\tfunc (s *jobSpawner) buildJobEnvBuilder(\n482\t\tctx context.Context,\n483\t\ttask lib.Task,\n484\t\tconfig pkg.AgentConfiguration,\n485\t) (k8s.EnvBuilder, error) {\n486\t\ttaskContent, err := renderTaskContent(ctx, task)\n487\t\tif err != nil {\n488\t\t\treturn nil, err\n489\t\t}\n490\t\tenvBuilder := k8s.NewEnvBuilder()\n491\t\tenvBuilder.Add(\"TASK_CONTENT\", taskContent)\n492\t\tenvBuilder.Add(\"TASK_ID\", string(task.TaskIdentifier))\n493\t\tenvBuilder.Add(\"KAFKA_BROKERS\", s.kafkaBrokers)\n494\t\tenvBuilder.Add(\"BRANCH\", s.branch)\n495\t\tenvBuilder.Add(\"TOPIC_PREFIX\", s.topicPrefix)\n496\t\tenvBuilder.Add(\"PHASE\", taskPhaseString(task.Frontmatter))\n497\t\tenvBuilder.Add(\"TASK_TYPE\", taskTypeString(task.Frontmatter))\n498\t\tfor key, value := range config.Env {\n499\t\t\tenvBuilder.Add(key, value)\n500\t\t}\n501\t\treturn envBuilder, nil\n502\t}\n503\t\n504\t// renderTaskContent serializes task into the markdown form an agent expects:\n505\t// \"---\\n<yaml-frontmatter>---\\n<body>\". When task.Frontmatter is empty, the\n506\t// body is returned unchanged (no empty \"{}\" wrapper).\n507\t//\n508\t// The agent side (lib.ParseMarkdown) reads frontmatter fields like\n509\t// clone_url / ref / base_ref directly from this string — keep the wrapper\n510\t// shape byte-compatible with controller/pkg/result.WriteResult.\n511\tfunc renderTaskContent(ctx context.Context, task lib.Task) (string, error) {\n512\t\tif len(task.Frontmatter) == 0 {\n513\t\t\treturn string(task.Content), nil\n514\t\t}\n515\t\tfmBytes, err := yaml.Marshal(map[string]any(task.Frontmatter))\n516\t\tif err != nil {\n517\t\t\treturn \"\", errors.Wrapf(ctx, err, \"marshal frontmatter for task %s\", task.TaskIdentifier)\n518\t\t}\n519\t\treturn \"---\\n\" + string(fmBytes) + \"---\\n\" + string(task.Content), nil\n520\t}\n521\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a883ed86f0a5df7d","session_id":"649c3b71-f1fd-46a9-a844-34ab62b5da5e","uuid":"c1a19274-0982-4a81-bd1f-5948c46abbf6","timestamp":"2026-09-03T17:46:27.739Z","subagent_type":"dark-factory:prompt-creator","task_description":"Generate prompts from spec 002"}
